---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { repository } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy and local recording · ScreenRecorder"
  description="Learn what ScreenRecorder records, where files and diagnostics are stored, which permissions it uses, and what the static website collects."
>
  <article class="guide wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href="/">← Home</a>
    <div class="eyebrow">PRIVACY · PRODUCT AND WEBSITE</div>
    <h1>
      Record locally.<br /><span class="accent-word">Know what leaves.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="guide-lead">
      ScreenRecorder is designed to save recordings on your device. It does not
      require an account or send recordings to a ScreenRecorder cloud service.
      This page describes the application and the separate public website.
    </p>

    <h2>ScreenRecorder application</h2>
    <p>
      Video and audio are written to the output location you choose. The app
      does not upload recordings to the project website. Windows uses the
      screen-capture APIs and audio inputs needed for the sources you enable.
      Fedora uses the desktop portal for screen or window capture and PipeWire
      audio sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      Camera, microphone, desktop audio and screen capture are used only for the
      recording features you enable. Your operating system may show its own
      permission or source-selection prompt.
    </p>

    <h2>Diagnostics and local files</h2>
    <p>
      Session diagnostics are stored locally to help investigate capture and
      encoding issues. They may include technical details such as timestamps,
      selected settings, encoder status and local output paths. Review a log
      before sharing it publicly, because paths can contain your account name or
      other personal information.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a recording is interrupted, a partial output may remain on disk. Keep
      it private unless you choose to share it; the app does not transmit it
      automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>Public website</h2>
    <p>
      This is a static website. It has no sign-in, user-submitted forms,
      advertising, or analytics script configured. The hosting provider may
      process connection data as part of serving pages. When you follow a link
      to GitHub, that service handles the visit under its own privacy terms.
    </p>
    <p>
      The website may be accessed by search and AI crawlers. Its
      <a href="/robots.txt">crawler policy</a> permits search, query-time AI use,
      and model-training use of the public website content.
    </p>

    <h2>Open source and contact</h2>
    <p>
      You can inspect the application source and report privacy concerns through
      the <a href={repository}>GitHub project</a>. This page applies to the
      currently published website and application behavior; it will be updated
      if those practices change.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
